Overall summary

I would word the overall assessment something like this:

Over the eight-week period, there was clear and sustained improvement across all four agreed areas: communication, project delivery, administration and ad-hoc work.

Communication was one of the key concerns at the outset. The expectation was for more active communication with stakeholders, including acknowledging requests where an immediate response was not possible and proactively communicating anything that could affect delivery. This improved significantly during the review period. Communication evidence was maintained, stakeholder relationships were managed appropriately, and no further communication concerns or complaints were raised.

Project delivery was also strong throughout the period. The employee increasingly discussed project work and potential issues with her manager rather than working in isolation. Work included SML queries, supporting the Rates team with multiple queries and Pay Transparency, which was delivered on time. Where workload prioritisation was required, this was discussed and support was provided, including reducing ServiceNow responsibilities where necessary to allow focus on project delivery. No project deadlines were missed during the period.

There was also a positive change in approach to ad-hoc work. At the beginning of the process, the employee questioned whether this type of work should form part of a Senior Analyst role. Following discussion that ServiceNow and ad-hoc requests were a shared responsibility across the team, she responded positively and changed her approach. She began proactively assigning herself tickets, supported delegation of work and ultimately became the highest contributor to ad-hoc work within the team during the period.

Administration and documentation expectations were also met. The employee maintained a OneNote record of her activities and achievements and created OneDrive folders containing evidence against the agreed measures. This supported greater visibility of her work and contribution.

Beyond the original measures, there was also evidence of increased contribution to the wider team. In the latter part of the period, she supported colleagues and delivered SQL, Workday and Workday Studio training sessions to help upskill the team.

Overall, the response to the eight-week plan was very positive. In particular, her attitude towards the feedback was constructive throughout. She engaged with the areas raised, demonstrated the required changes and sustained them across the review period. At the final meeting, I confirmed that I was very pleased with the progress made and particularly impressed with the way she had responded to the feedback. There were no outstanding performance concerns from my perspective at the end of the eight weeks. We agreed that regular 1:1 meetings would continue and that a summary of the eight-week period would be provided before considering any further next Week

Main focus / progress

Week 1

Initial discussion and four measures agreed. Discussed active stakeholder communication, acknowledging requests even where an immediate answer was unavailable. Discussed expectations around project delivery and escalating/communicating potential delays. Admin expectations included keeping user guides updated and documenting achievements/contributions. Clarified that ad-hoc work was a shared team responsibility regardless of seniority. Employee was positive about having a clear plan and engaged constructively.

Week 2

Early positive progress. Began maintaining a OneNote record of work/actions and proactively taking ServiceNow ad-hoc tickets. Four OneDrive evidence folders were requested to capture evidence against the agreed measures. Communication with stakeholders began improving. Employee also started discussing project work more proactively with manager.

Week 3

Continued improvement. Positive stakeholder communication observed, including around SML (Starters, Movers and Leavers) work. Increased communication with manager regarding project work and potential issues. Continued proactively assigning ServiceNow tickets. Positive feedback given on maintaining OneNote, increased communication and proactive approach to team workload.

Week 4

Midpoint review. Significant improvement noted across the agreed measures. No stakeholder concerns or missed deadlines identified. Communication was significantly improved and project delivery remained on track. Evidence folders were being maintained and reviewed. No new performance concerns raised.

Week 5

Progress maintained. Continued timely delivery of project and ad-hoc work. Employee proactively picked up ServiceNow requests and increasingly supported distribution/delegation of work across the team. Manager continued to support workload prioritisation, including removing/reducing ServiceNow work where project priorities required additional focus.

Week 6

Strong performance continued with no stakeholder issues or missed deadlines. Work included SML queries, supporting the Rates team with multiple queries and Pay Transparency work. Evidence demonstrated continued communication and ad-hoc contribution. Employee was increasingly contributing beyond her own immediate workload and supporting colleagues.

Week 7

Performance remained strong across all four measures. Employee demonstrated increased senior-level contribution through supporting colleagues and delivering knowledge-sharing/upskilling sessions, including SQL, Workday and Workday Studio training. Stakeholder management and delivery remained positive with no concerns raised.

Week 8

Final review. No outstanding concerns identified. All four areas showed sustained improvement. Communication with stakeholders was effective, deadlines were met, admin/documentation expectations were completed, and ad-hoc contribution had changed substantially from the initial position—employee became the highest contributor to ad-hoc work within the team. Positive feedback given on her attitude throughout the eight weeks and how constructively she responded to feedback. Agreed to continue through normal 1:1 meetings, with an overall summary to be provided and next steps considered.

